# TODO - Mutation pacing and human checkpoints

## Objective

Share bounded account mutation budgets across transports and preserve
recoverable human-action stops for challenges and unfamiliar access states.

## Acceptance

**IT WORKS** when actual publication operations share a conservative account
mutation queue, respect a usable pace/budget, respond to cancellation, and stop
for authentication challenges or other human actions. An interruption or
restart must not blindly repeat an uncertain Blooket write; resumption uses
fresh session and remote state.

Verify the ordinary product path and at least one representative interrupted or
cancelled flow with focused deterministic tests. A bounded, recoverable stop is
a working outcome where the provider requires human attention. Do not delay
functional publication for an exhaustive theoretical interleaving matrix; keep
finding and fixing additional edge cases in the ongoing hardening task.

## Implementation notes

The user's request concerns Blooket interaction timing, not the MCP's writing
style. Navigate, fill, and submit in the normal browser sequence; wait for page
readiness and confirmation between actions instead of issuing a burst of work.
Apply the same conservative pacing to admitted HTTP actions.

Use one shared per-account mutation queue across MCP, CLI, and local UI, with
only one write in flight. Proposed application defaults are at least two seconds
between mutation starts and at most 20 starts in a rolling minute. These are
application choices, not verified Blooket limits or a guarantee against account
restrictions; use stricter verified provider limits when known.

Persisted execution can now accept one shared cancellable pacer. It acquires
after session and verification baseline admission, then durably reserves the
plan-bound task budget before opening the existing attempt journal and remote
mutation. Session/human stops consume neither pacing nor budget. Ambiguous
mutations retain the consumed budget start, and budget exhaustion stops before a
journal or remote write.

The durable budget adapter is lock-protected, plan-bound, strictly decoded, and
atomically persisted; restart reloads the same start count and deadline. The
actual CLI, MCP and UI publication commands now share the running service's
publication pacer. Owning-surface acceptance remains pending for uninterrupted
multi-question progression, configurable limits and cancellation/recovery.

After a pacing lease is granted, persisted execution now re-observes the browser
session without authenticating and then re-reads the verification baseline
before budget reservation or journaling. Session expiry, rate limiting, human
challenges, malformed baseline evidence, or a changed remote digest release the
lease and stop without consuming the task budget or opening a mutation journal.

Make pacing, mutations per minute, task mutation budget, and maximum task
duration configurable within bounded ranges. Persist progress and yield with a
clear status when a task budget is exhausted; do not silently drop operations.
Keep pause/cancel responsive and apply provider retry guidance when available.

Count attempts as well as confirmed writes, prevent parallel clients from
evading the shared limit, and never retry or switch transports after an
ambiguous mutation without reconciliation. Stop on rate-limit or challenge
states rather than accelerating, guessing success, or hiding automation.

If a CAPTCHA or security challenge appears during the local browser workflow,
preserve the checkpoint, return `human-action-required`, and show the actual
page/origin that needs attention. Let the teacher complete the challenge locally
and resume only after observing legitimate completion. The user has not observed
a CAPTCHA; do not invent one or add a localhost CAPTCHA merely for first-use
diagnostics.

Any assistant attempt to solve a displayed CAPTCHA requires the user's explicit
confirmation at that moment; a general request to finish the workflow is not
advance approval. Provide assistance only within that confirmed interaction.
Never bypass the challenge, use solving services, forge validation, transfer
challenge/session secrets through MCP, or suppress unfamiliar login stops.

## Evidence

A red-to-green Add Question continuation regression covers two more
provider/Chrome hydration races. A newly navigated target may return no native
document origin for several bounded observations, or the native Edit document
may appear before React classifies it as Edit. The host now waits for these
transitions only after a proven host-owned navigation or reload, and never
opens a modal before verified edit-page readiness.

The wait pins the first document lifetime observed during an unclassified
post-navigation shell. A same-route document replacement during that interval
fails before any modal open or Save; an existing unexpected teacher-selected
route still fails immediately. Persistent unknown shells exhaust the original
50-poll budget. Portable host tests prove one maximum Save for recovered
hydration and zero Save for drift, replacement or failed readiness; this does
not establish a new live question receipt.

The owner-safe local launcher additionally recognizes a terminated Linux
process that is temporarily retained as a zombie by the host. The service's
exact prior PID, removed discovery and lock files, drained writer leases and
refused loopback listener are still all required before stop can be confirmed.
A real standalone development-service stop had previously returned
`service-stop-unconfirmed` despite having drained; the user service was
immediately restored with the official supervised launcher.

Synthetic state parsing and live-process checks now cover this distinction
without loosening macOS or other platform rules. This does not certify
future host shutdowns.

The restart-safe account pacing ledger was hardened further after a
red-to-green JSON-format regression: duplicate, reordered or noncanonical
fields now fail closed even when `JSON.parse` alone would admit them. Descriptor
reads also compare actual bytes and file timestamps to detect concurrent edits.
Owner-only `0600` output, released lock files and symbolic lock refusals have
dedicated portable regressions. No private account write was attempted.

On 2026-10-14, the per-data-root rolling account pacing ledger is now
durable across service restarts rather than only in memory. The normal
server-owned pacer commits recent start timestamps in an atomic owner-only
`mutation-pacing.json` before returning its mutation lease. The file holds
only epoch millisecond timestamps and contains no account IDs, quiz content,
provider data or credentials.

The adapter locks each reservation and refuses malformed, oversized, symlinked
or unreadable evidence. A stricter policy continues to count preceding starts,
and a wall-clock rollback fails closed. Cancellation while waiting holds no
new reservation. Unknown storage status returns a pre-write pacing failure
without task budget consumption, attempt journal or provider call.

Synthetic regressions cover process restart, 2-second and rolling-minute
limits, simultaneous writers, policy getters and symbols, invalid clocks,
corrupted files, and service boot preserving existing history unchanged.
The actual Chrome service remains independent of the synthetic provider tests;
no old ambiguous write was replayed. Live multi-question acceptance remains
pending and this TODO stays open.

An additional read-only editor regression verifies that prompt, timer and
Multiple Choice shuffle controls retain their observed element types and
required enabled state after React preparation. Synthetic tag/type changes or
disabled shuffle controls previously could pass the final visible echo;
the pre-Save guard now stops them without issuing another mutation.

On 2026-10-14, canonical publication now snapshots and validates any
caller-supplied pacing policy before reading drafts or opening a journal.
Malformed, accessor-backed or unknown policy fields cannot trigger a remote
write. A resumed synthetic two-step publication also proves that raising a
previously exhausted start limit advances the original checkpoint exactly once
and verifies without replaying Create Set.

The last read-only Multiple Choice pre-submit check additionally requires all
four observed correctness controls to remain unique, visible and enabled.
Red-to-green regressions cover missing, duplicate and disabled toggles while
leaving the native provider-serialized answer semantics authoritative. These
checks do not establish live automatic Add Question completion.

The version-four publication-policy decoder now owns only exact enumerable
data fields. Red-to-green tests prove that accessor properties are not invoked,
and symbol-owned and inherited policy keys cannot relax application pacing.
Unknown fields still fail closed; no account state was inspected or changed.

A follow-up canonical publication fixture seeds an expired, otherwise valid
durable budget and applies the configured one-minute duration. The next step
returns `mutation-task-duration-exhausted` without touching the prior budget
bytes, opening an attempt journal or contacting Blooket. UI error labels now
distinguish configured budget exhaustion from provider rate limiting and never
suggest automatic retries. This is synthetic acceptance only.

On 2026-10-14, publication pacing gained persisted version-four teacher
preferences and local Settings controls. Four exact bounded values now govern
the running service's shared CLI/MCP/UI mutation-start queue and canonical
task budget. A policy is fixed for the service lifetime; changed settings take
effect after restart, not mid-journal. Legacy v3 settings load with safe
defaults and migrate on explicit save without deleting the previous file.

Portable regressions prove invalid and fractional limits are rejected, unit
conversions are exact, stale v3 writes cannot clear stricter policies, settings
survive service restart, and a configured one-start task stops before a second
journal or Blooket call. The live Add Question multi-question acceptance and
provider pacing limits remain unverified, so this TODO stays open.

On 2026-10-14, an additional red-to-green pacing regression found that
mutating a caller-owned configuration object after queue construction could
accelerate the already admitted start interval or rolling-minute allowance.
The queue now captures and freezes its validated policy using exact own data
properties, rejects getters and unknown shapes, and freezes the exported
default. These checks are independent of the separately durable task budget.

On 2026-10-14, the Add Question host gained a separate read-only
pre-submit echo check after React-control preparation and before the one
latched Save. Typing and Multiple Choice fixtures reproduce a late provider
rewrite of visible prompt, answers, match mode or shuffle that leaves the
hidden JSON unchanged. The host now refuses a mismatched echo and never clicks
Save or reloads in that case; malformed Chrome acknowledgements fail closed.

This is a portable regression, not an automatic receipt for the already
ambiguous live Add Question attempts. The connected extension was not
reloaded over their unsaved editor; those journals remain unreplayed.

On 2026-10-14, a separate read-only extension-world diagnostic was added
for unresolved Add Question stages. It reports only a fixed enum for modal
ownership, form detachment, React control preparation, finalization,
interruption or a latched submit attempt. It does not return IDs, questions,
answers, URLs or captured provider data and cannot authorize a fresh Save.

Synthetic tests distinguish a possible clicked Save from a detached form,
prevent getter evaluation, and verify that the diagnostic is self-contained
for Chrome's isolated scripting world. The existing unresolved form was not
reloaded, dismissed or mutated to test it.

On 2026-10-14, red-to-green tests found that a wall-clock rollback during
mutation pacing could create a much longer wait than the configured 2-second
interval. Nonfinite timestamps could also enter the wait loop instead of
failing. The in-process service queue now uses a monotonic elapsed-time clock,
while the separately persisted task-duration budget retains its strict
wall-clock rollback admission checks.

The pacer also validates every injected clock read, including after a wait and
at final lease grant. Invalid timestamps fail before mutation admission;
exclusive queue slots are released and a later valid acquisition can proceed.
Ten deterministic tests cover serialization, the rolling limit, cancellation,
clock rollback and recovery. No provider mutation or user journal was used.

A fresh owner-authorized, private synthetic one-question publication on
2026-10-14 automatically confirmed Create Set after the newly compiled
companion was reloaded into the existing Chrome for Testing profile.
The question write then returned `write-not-confirmed` with checkpoint 1/2.
No second Save or manual receipt adoption was attempted.

A read-only browser check found the owned question form still open with the
expected Typing prompt, answer, match mode and 15-second limit in both visible
controls and the provider-generated hidden JSON. The exact fresh question read
failed, so no automatic question receipt or final publication verification
was claimed. The original unrelated ambiguous attempt remained unreplayed.

Additional red-to-green fixtures show the hidden provider state may hydrate
before the visible picker and text fields. Bounded readiness polling now waits
for uniquely owned controls without submitting, adopting another document, or
relaxing human-stop checks. Real multi-question progression remains open.

On 2026-10-14, a read-only Chrome inspection identified why a canonical
Add Question attempt could save empty data: changing the hidden serialized
question bypassed React's visible editor state, which replaced it on Save.
The browser question adapter now fills only the provider's visible controls,
checks the resulting native serialization, and never overwrites the hidden
field. Single-shot form ownership, human-interaction invalidation, and fresh
remote post-Save verification remain required.

Portable serialized Chrome and DOM fixtures cover Typing match modes, four
Multiple Choice answers, shuffle, disabled or ambiguous controls, and late
initial React hydration. The preparation gate checks exact semantic fields,
rejects unknown fields and noncanonical JSON, and never treats hidden state as
a remote receipt. These tests are not a new live multi-question confirmation.

On 2026-10-14, two red-to-green Add Question regressions reproduced premature
stops after one Save: an unknown transitional Edit shell and a verification
reload that still exposed the old document or no document origin. The host
now waits within its existing post-submit/read budget, without resubmitting
or accepting the old document as fresh proof.

The verification reload carries its prior native origin into the private
waiter, and only its new document can authorize saved read-back. Unknown
post-save state is bounded; explicit security/organization stops remain prompt,
and pre-mutation admission is unchanged. All 61 Add Question host tests passed,
including persistence, one-submit counts, fresh read-back and human-stop cases.
Live automatic multi-question confirmation remains pending.

On 2026-10-14, the actual CLI/UI publication path created and verified a
private one-question quiz through the shared server-owned pacer. Create Set
confirmed automatically, while an uncertain Add Question result was recovered
by read-only reconciliation without resubmission; final fresh verification
and a subsequent question read both succeeded.

Ordinary Add Question confirmation still needs its post-save readiness issue
resolved. Its visible saved question and recoverable journal do not establish
reliable automatic progression through several questions. This record owns
that bounded confirmation timing alongside queue, cancellation and budget
acceptance; publication media/editing keep their separate ownership.

On 2026-10-14, the local Quizzes UI began using the existing server-owned
shared publication pacer and journaled command boundary. It dispatches a
single step at a time and checks the durable operation count before another.
Explicit cancellation interrupts the active request and prevents later steps.
A reconciliation-required result stops the UI; only a separate human-selected
read-only reconcile request can inspect it without submitting another write.

Portable UI tests cover cancellation after a confirmed step, uncertain writes,
changed plan lengths and nonadvancing results. These are not live cancellation
or external-provider pacing acceptance, so this task remains open.

On 2026-10-10, the durable mutation-budget reader began opening the exact
local file descriptor with `O_NOFOLLOW` before inspecting or reading it.
Previously an `lstat` followed by a pathname-based `readFile` permitted a
symlink replacement between checks.

The new bounded descriptor read rejects symlink aliases, unreadable or
nonregular files, and growth beyond 4,096 bytes. Synthetic filesystem
regressions cover dangling and ordinary symlinks, budget reservation refusal,
and the exact file-size boundary. No live provider write
or exploit timing is claimed.

On 2026-10-08, persisted write execution added a second cancellation
checkpoint immediately after durable budget reservation. An abort while the
budget adapter is working now prevents journal creation and remote mutation,
although the already-persisted budget start remains conservatively consumed.
A deterministic regression aborts during the injected reservation clock and
verifies the missing journal, absent remote call, and released pacing lease.

On 2026-10-08, the in-process mutation pacer was hardened so an aborted
reservation can complete while an earlier lease is still held. Its reserved
queue slot still resolves only after the incumbent, preventing a later caller
from overlapping the exclusive mutation window. Injected-clock regressions
verify prompt cancellation, unchanged start counts, and serial execution with
two independently canceled queued reservations.

A portable in-process pacing foundation was added on 2026-10-06. It grants one
exclusive mutation lease at a time, enforces the two-second and 20-per-minute
defaults with an injected clock, permits only stricter configuration, and
cancels queued work without consuming a start.

A strict task-budget state counts attempted mutation starts with bounded
maximum-start and maximum-duration policies. Its value object now lives with the
write-plan domain, while a dedicated adapter reserves the next state atomically
under a file lock. Tests cover restart, exhaustion, clock rollback, plan
mismatch, symlinks, malformed/oversized files, lock contention, cancellation,
and serialization across distinct persisted write paths.

## Blockers

Acceptance remains open until the checks above run through the actual owning
product surfaces; portable foundations alone do not complete this record.
